Choose execution from extracted dependencies. Execute only coupled steps in the main session. When the plan declares independent units, dispatch ready units concurrently to sub-agents. Respect dependencies, collect each unit's implementation and verification proof, then integrate and run cross-unit and full verification in the main session. Never let two agents edit the same file; keep integration, final verification, and artifact submission in the main session.

Ownership may come from a unit's exact `Paths:`, its `Directories:` (the declared directory limits), or its steps' `Files:` when the unit has neither. Do not broaden file ownership to a parent directory. Remove assignments to `.agent`, `.git`, and `.worktrees` (and descendants) before making a worker brief. An unknown or empty effective scope, malformed dependency graph, or unextractable ownership stays in the main session; it never grants broad worker writes.

A worker cap limits concurrent units, not total units: queue later ready units in waves. Serialize conflicting ownership—equal files, directory ancestry, and directory/file containment conflict—until the earlier writer finishes. Units with disjoint file ownership in one directory may proceed together. Recompute readiness after each result; list order alone is not a dependency.

For every dispatched unit, give the worker its unit ID, assigned steps, exact effective ownership, focused verification, and the brokered-tools-only rule. Workers do not commit, integrate, or submit the main artifact. Their result is a lead the main session checks before final reconciliation. Raw plan prose remains authoritative: retain all residual prose work and unowned global steps in the main session, even when extraction cannot represent them as worker assignments.

### Recovering from helper failures and bridging gaps locally

When a sub-agent returns an unsuccessful or unusable result, do not let the ready scope stall. On helper failure, inspect evidence and choose an evidence-backed changed tactic: correct the brief, dispatch a different exposed helper, or perform the ready scope locally in this session. Before transferring ownership, confirm the previous writer has stopped — never assume a worker has released a path until its run reports terminated, and never start a parallel writer on the same files. Continue owned ready work when slots are saturated: when every dispatch slot is busy and independent ready work is still waiting, finish that scope locally instead of idling on the dispatch pipeline. A ready reference that no worker is taking is still the main session's responsibility.
